## What the scan actually says

Three findings, but only **two distinct edges** in the graph:

| Edge | Finding | Legal? |
|---|---|---|
| `application/billing-flow.ts:1` → `domain/billing.ts` | — | ✅ legal |
| `presentation/invoice-view.ts:3` → `application/billing-flow.ts` | — | ✅ legal (no rule bars presentation → application) |
| `domain/billing.ts:2` → `presentation/invoice-view.ts` | **crime_00002** (layer_violation) | ❌ `domain cannotImport presentation` |
| `some-pkg/dist/internal/private/helper.js` @ `billing-flow.ts:4` | **crime_00003** (deep_import) | ❌ past the package surface |

The cycle in **crime_00001** (`application → domain → presentation → application`) is not an independent problem. It is closed by exactly one edge — the illegal one. Break `domain/billing.ts:2` and crime_00001 and crime_00002 both retire.

**Also worth knowing before you touch it:** this isn't just a static smell. `renderInvoice` → `runBillingFlow` → `chargeCustomer` → `renderInvoice` is unbounded recursion at runtime. The cycle finding is describing live stack-overflow behaviour, not only a tree-shaking hazard.

## Change 1 — redirect, don't delete (crime_00002 + crime_00001)

`domain/billing.ts` currently *renders*, which is why it needs presentation. Invert it: domain returns a value, presentation formats it.

```ts
// src/domain/billing.ts — leaf, no imports
export interface Charge { amount: number }
export function chargeCustomer(amount: number): Charge {
  return { amount };
}
```
```ts
// src/application/billing-flow.ts
import { chargeCustomer, type Charge } from "../domain/billing.js";
export function runBillingFlow(amount: number): Charge {
  return chargeCustomer(amount);
}
```
```ts
// src/presentation/invoice-view.ts
import { runBillingFlow } from "../application/billing-flow.js";
export function renderInvoice(amount: number): string {
  return `Invoice: ${runBillingFlow(amount).amount}`;
}
```

Remaining edges: `presentation → application → domain`. Acyclic, and legal under both configured rules.

The finding's own `suggested_action` offers the alternative — a port owned by domain (`interface InvoiceRenderer` in domain, implementation injected from presentation). That's the right shape when domain genuinely must trigger rendering. Here it doesn't; it only needed a string. Prefer deleting the dependency over inverting it — a port you don't need is architecture theatre, and it leaves the runtime recursion in place unless you're careful.

## Change 2 — break to the package boundary (crime_00003)

`billing-flow.ts:4` should import `some-pkg`'s documented entry:

```ts
import { helper } from "some-pkg";
```

If the symbol isn't exported there, don't code around it in place. Put the deep specifier behind one adapter — `src/infrastructure/some-pkg-adapter.ts` — so the next upgrade breaks exactly one file, and file the upstream issue. Note the layer rules permit this: only `domain` is barred from `infrastructure`; `application` may import it.

`effort: "quick"`, `risk: "low"` — do this one first as a standalone commit, since it's independent of the restructure above.

## How the two differ in agent risk

Scores: layer_violation `0.34`, deep_import `0.29`. The gap is small but the failure modes are qualitatively different, and that's the part that matters.

**layer_violation is a silent constraint.** The rule lives in `crimes.config.json`, not in the code. Nothing in `domain/billing.ts` tells an agent that reaching into presentation is forbidden — the import resolves, types check, tests pass. An agent asked to "add tax to the charge" will read the file, see `renderInvoice` already imported, and reuse it. Every such edit is locally well-reasoned and globally wrong, and the only feedback loop is a human noticing months later. It also inverts the direction of change: once domain depends on presentation, "tweak the invoice format" has a blast radius that includes billing rules. Confidence `0.95` — this is a declared rule with a declared breach, not a heuristic.

**deep_import is a loud constraint.** The specifier is self-documenting; `some-pkg/dist/internal/private/helper.js` announces its own crime in the source line. The failure is external and temporal — the next dependency bump moves the file and resolution fails at build time. That's fail-fast: an agent gets a hard error, not silent drift. Confidence `0.85` is lower because the heuristic can't distinguish a private path from a package that legitimately publishes subpaths.

So the ranking reflects **detectability by the next agent**, not blast radius: both findings report `blast_radius_importers: 3` (this fixture is four files — don't read much into that number). Layer violations degrade an agent's model of the codebase; deep imports just break, visibly, later.

**Order of work:** Change 2 first (quick, isolated), then Change 1 as one commit that closes both crime_00001 and crime_00002. Re-run `crimes scan --format json` after; a clean report is the acceptance test.
